<section class="page-hero"><p class="eyebrow"><span></span>Founder</p><h1>AAB began with a simple concern: valuable learning disappears.</h1><p>Projects finish. People move on. Failures are buried. Evidence becomes separated from the reasoning that gave it meaning. AAB is being built to change that.</p></section>
<section class="launch-section"><div class="founder-layout"><aside class="founder-note"><strong>“Science should remember not only what worked, but why it was believed, what challenged it and what remains unknown.”</strong><p>This principle shaped AAB’s commitment to scientific memory, traceable evidence and explicit human authority.</p></aside><div class="founder-copy"><p class="eyebrow dark"><span></span>Why AAB exists</p><h2>Understanding should survive the project that produced it.</h2><p>David Gorey founded AAB around the belief that scientific value is lost when organisations preserve conclusions but not the evidence, contradictions, failures, uncertainty and unanswered questions surrounding them.</p><p>Agriculture became the first proving domain because its learning is inseparable from field reality: formulation lineage, trials, plots, environmental context, observations, outcomes and accountable scientific review.</p><p>The longer-term ambition reaches beyond agriculture. AAB is intended to become governed scientific memory and reasoning infrastructure that can support water, environment, aquaculture, food systems, manufacturing, medicine and research—without pretending that software replaces scientific authority.</p><div class="founder-signoff"><strong>David Gorey</strong><span>Founder, AAB</span></div></div></div></section>
